{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"manapouri","name":"Manapouri","operator":"Meridian Energy","note":"Lake Manapouri discharges through the power station and tailrace tunnels to Doubtful Sound. The Mararoa Weir turns the Mararoa and the upper Waiau into the lake; the lower Waiau below it carries a residual flow. The Te Anau control gates regulate the upper Waiau between Lakes Te Anau and Manapouri.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroelectric_power_in_New_Zealand"]},{"id":"monowai","name":"Monowai","operator":"Pioneer Energy","note":"Lake Monowai outlet control and a canal to the power station near the Waiau. EA Report 4 (3.3, p. 19, Figure 3-5): gates at the outlet control the lake, then \"Water flows down the river for about 6 km into a lake formed by a weir across the river\" and an 856 m canal and a 1,036 m pipeline take it to the powerhouse, so only the river below the weir is bypassed (OSM maps the weir at -45.77995, 167.59121, at the head of the canal). Not modelled that way (rf-6u2): the 8 m network routes the lake's outflow north into the Borland Burn instead of down the Monowai (REC2), so the marking still starts at the outlet and follows the network's path.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf"]}],"r":[[5635,1,0,97],[5636,1,0,97],[5654,1,0,97],[5661,1,0,97],[5662,1,0,97],[5701,1,0,97],[5703,1,0,97],[5704,1,0